!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/invalid/a-definition-mark-is-not-an-annotation-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2, [TSON-SCHEMA] §6, [TSON-SCHEMA] §12.1"
  description: """
    How a record may be realised is written as a word after `=>`, never as an annotation. §6 fixes the
    home of a fact -- an annotation is the right home exactly when the mark changes no value's validity
    -- and this one changes it: erase it and a direct instance becomes readable at every position typed
    by the record.

    The name is refused rather than ignored, which is the whole point of the vector. A processor that
    merely stopped reading `@abstract` would resolve it against the governing meta, park it in the
    author-annotation channel, and leave the record OPEN -- silently admitting the values the author
    wrote the mark to exclude. Refusing by name is unconditional and happens before the meta is
    consulted, so no meta-schema can give the name a second meaning at a declaration.
    """
  meta: "meta.tn"
  import: ["core.tn"]
  error: {
    category: resolver
  }
}
